our phones can be a double-edged sword  they allow us to have our groceries be  brought right to our door and pay bills  without ever needing to drive anywhere  but they can also be time sucking  vampires that Rob us of precious moments  in life  and while they're meant to improve our  productivity  often the constant beeps and  never-ending scrolling end up doing  exactly the opposite  and we become ever distracted and find  it difficult to be productive hey guys  I'm Jesse and today I'm going to be  sharing with you how I organize my phone  in a way that helps minimize  distractions and improve productivity so  I'll share with you the 10 tips that I  use to help me use my phone in a way  that adds value to my life rather than  create addictive phone habits this is  actually a two-part video so if you want  to see how I actually organize my phone  to keep it minimal and clutter free and  I'll leave that video down in the  description box below and if you want to  see more videos on minimalism and living  life more fully with less be sure to  subscribe for more positive videos like  these tip number one is to declutter  your apps the first thing that I did  when I purchased my new phone is that I  went in and I deleted any of the apps  that came with the phone that I knew I  wasn't going to be using so I recommend  that you go through your phone and  declutter any of the unnecessary apps  and if you find yourself thinking huh I  didn't even know I had this then it's  probably a good sign that you don't need  it on your phone to begin with and this  is a big one declutter any of those apps  that become addictive for you and by  this I mean those apps that basically  suck up your time without giving  anything back in return for example if  you know that social media is one of  your biggest time suckers consider  taking a social media fast where you  delete the app for a certain period of  time let's say a week or a month and  then just see how you feel after that  period of time you may find that you  feel better off without it and the same  goes for any buying or purchasing apps  years back in an effort to reduce my  online shopping I deleted all of my  online shopping apps so basically for me  my biggest culprits were Amazon and  Target and by deleting these I kind of  created a barrier to help with impulse  shopping if you struggle with impulse  shopping just doing this alone can be a  huge help because this now makes it so  that you actually have to open up a tab  and log in and search for the item and  put in your card before purchasing an  item where before it was just easy you  just type it in because it's already on  there everything's already loaded you  may find that just doing the simple  thing can really go a long way to help  you reduce any of that boredom or just  random shopping that you didn't really  need to do tip number two is huge and it  is to disable notifications whenever and  wherever you can I have disabled  notifications on all apps except for of  course phone calls and messages but for  me Duolingo which is where I have my  daily French lessons and I want it to  notify me because it lets me know when I  have missed a lesson so I kind of want  to be nagged at for accountability and  just to create a positive habit but for  those apps that you know you should be  spending less time on or that you want  to spend less time on go in and disable  notifications or at least alter them in  a way that they are less distracting so  that you can still know that you have a  notification but it won't actually Buzz  or beep or try to get your attention  often those beeps and buzzes are the  biggest culprits that throw off our  Focus tip number three is easily one of  my favorites and it is to have a home  for your phone this is something that I  implemented after having kids because it  became so important for me to be able to  have one-on-one time without a phone  physically in front of my screen just to  help me become less attached to this  device because unfortunately the great  majority of people have like a mini  panic attack the moment they realize  their phone is missing because we're  just so used to it being attached to our  bodies whether in a purse in a pocket or  under a pillow which I know is not you  guys right so physically creating a  barrier or having a designated place  where your phone stays when it's not in  use can really help to kind of ease that  separation anxiety so that way you can  get to a point where you can go  throughout your whole day without even  realizing that your phone is missing for  me I place mine in this mail holder that  lives right at the front door  so this way if I get a call or I need it  it's still handy but it's also out of  sight out of mind tip number four is to  sleep several feet away from your phone  if you want to be more productive and  get things done you have got to place a  high priority on your sleeping habits  and making sure that you sleep well at  night I can't tell you the number of  times that I've pushed myself to get a  project done sacrificing sleep in the  process only to end up sick and having  to recover for the next few days so if  you want to improve productivity sleep  is crucial but having your phone right  beside you or underneath your pillow is  not going to help you to sleep well at  night for one the blue light lowers your  body's ability to produce melatonin and  two it just makes it much more likely  that you're going to be getting  distracted if it's in close proximity to  you and you just have to kind of rage  over just to check one more thing before  you go to bed and that one more thing  suddenly becomes two or three hours of  scrolling and by the way if you want to  hear more about how to create a better  sleep routine so that you can be more  productive effective check out my video  on how to wake up at 5am I'll leave that  video down in the description box below  tip number five in order to help you  reduce visual clutter and minimize  distractions use a calming image or even  a blank solid image as your background  color and this can also help you save  battery life I used to swap out my  backgrounds all the time just like  social media had trained me to do and  now I have a landscape photo that is  Serene and calming to look at and maybe  I'm a mean mom because I don't  constantly change out my background for  pictures of my kids or my husband but  you know what I choose to look at them  in person and for me that's really the  whole point it's about knowing what  actually matters to you and reducing  those things that keep you from living  life that you want to live so that you  can truly live and Savor life rather  than just going through the motions tip  number six is to keep only the basics on  your main screen so think of the apps  that you use more frequently and no I  don't mean the addictive apps by keeping  only those necessary items or just the  main things on your main screen it makes  it less likely that you're going to get  distracted by a little notification or  just the desire to check on another app  this way if you go to place a phone call  it just makes it much more likely that  that's the only thing that you're going  to do tip number seven is to disable  raise to wake one of the things that you  will notice with my phone is that it  does not automatically turn on when the  screen is raised  I've purposely turned off this feature  to minimize distractions  so this basically just makes it so that  I actually have to tap or physically  click on the screen in order for the  screen to activate so this way if I  happen to be moving my phone around  without actually having the intention to  use it I'm not going to be distracted by  the phone subliminally telling me look  at me tip number eight hands off utilize  the Siri or Google or Alexa or whatever  you have that can allow you to speak to  your phone rather than being physically  in front of your phone I think I've said  this before but the more hands off you  can be with your phone the better  because anytime you go to touch your  phone it's going to be much more likely  that you're going to do something else  other than what you intended to do I  love using the Siri function I can use  it to send messages to place phone calls  to turn on and off our smart bulbs or  schedule appointments and doing this  makes it so that I can use it when it's  in my little cubby and I don't have to  actually go get my phone or I don't have  to be in front of this screen and then  face the possibility that I'm going to  get distracted by doing something else  the more hands off you can be with your  phone the better tip number nine is  super simple super fast but it can also  help you save on battery life and it is  to go through and delete all of your  search tabs message threads voicemails  calls Etc sometimes we don't even  realize that we have like years worth of  messages on our phones that we don't  really need it's just one more thing to  look at one more thing to get distracted  by so doing this can be a quick little  thing to do that can save your battery  life and just have less to look at less  visual clutter and lastly number 10 use  sleep mode this is one of my favorite  hacks for improving productivity and  minimizing distractions set a sleep mode  to automatically turn on around the time  when you usually go to bed I've  mentioned that I work as a home nurse  and unfortunately my employer does not  provide work phones so I constantly get  messages about work all the time so I  have my phone set to automatically  silence messages and calls at night and  what I love about this is that you can  set specific people on your favorites  that can call you on your notify you if  you want them to so for me for example  they have to call me twice in order for  the phone call to go through and I did  this specifically because for example  when John was a newborn and sleep was  such a precious commodity If I  miraculously got both kids to nap at the  same time the last thing that I wanted  was for my mom to call me just to check  up on me and for the call to go through  so in this case what it meant is that I  had to let people know if you want to  reach me and it's truly an emergency  call me twice I really recommend you  have a set schedule at night that's  automatic that you don't have to think  about but if you have a project that you  really want to focus on utilize it  during the day as well don't let people  invade on your time and energy when  you're really trying to focus on  something that truly matters to you if  you can take anything from this video  let it be this if you don't respect your  time no one else will that's all I have  for today's video I hope that it was  helpful for you if so please give it a  thumbs up and subscribe so you won't  miss when I post a new video I thank you  so much for watching and I'll see you in  the next one bye for now
 

